Key finding: drivers/media/usb/cx231xx/cx231xx-417.c:1341-1359 cx231xx_bulk_copy — uses urb->actual_length with no bound check; short completion (0-2 bytes) wraps buffer_size-3 (u32) to ~4GB memcpy → OOB read; kmalloc(buffer_size). This is the same pattern as s05 (reported high confidence, line 1341-1359) and s02. In s09 I confirmed the code at lines 1341-1360.

Let me get exact line numbers for the quote. From my earlier read: sed -n '1300,1360p' showed:

```
static int cx231xx_bulk_copy(struct cx231xx *dev, struct urb *urb)
{
	struct cx231xx_dmaqueue *dma_q = urb->context;
	unsigned char *p_buffer, *buffer;
	u32 buffer_size = 0;

	p_buffer = urb->transfer_buffer;
	buffer_size = urb->actual_length;

	buffer = kmalloc(buffer_size, GFP_ATOMIC);
	if (!buffer)
		return -ENOMEM;

	memcpy(buffer, dma_q->ps_head, 3);
	memcpy(buffer+3, p_buffer, buffer_size-3);
	memcpy(dma_q->ps_head, p_buffer+buffer_size-3, 3);

	p_buffer = buffer;
	buffer_filled(p_buffer, buffer_size, urb, dma_q);
```

Line numbers: grep showed line 1341 = "static int cx231xx_bulk_copy", 1348 = buffer_size = urb->actual_length, 1350 = kmalloc, 1355 = memcpy(buffer+3...), 1356 = memcpy ps_head, 1359 = buffer_filled.

Note: for bulk URB, HCD guarantees actual_length <= transfer_buffer_length = sb_size = 320 * max_pkt_size (from init_bulk called with max_packets=320). So normal case: buffer_size <= sb_size. The vulnerability: no check buffer_size >= 3. If device completes a bulk-in with 0, 1, or 2 bytes (short transfer — device firmware controlled), buffer_size-3 as u32 underflows: buffer_size-3 = 0xFFFFFFFD (or 0 for 3? if buffer_size==3 then buffer_size-3=0, fine). With buffer_size = 0: kmalloc(0) returns ZERO_SIZE_PTR (non-NULL!) → memcpy(buffer, ps_head, 3) writes 3 bytes to ZERO_SIZE_PTR → null-ish page write (actually ZERO_SIZE_PTR is 0x10, so writing to 0x10 — invalid). Wait: kmalloc(0, GFP_ATOMIC) returns ZERO_SIZE_PTR = ((void *)16), and the `if (!buffer)` check passes since ZERO_SIZE_PTR != NULL. Then memcpy(buffer, ps_head, 3) writes to address 0x10 → crash. And memcpy(buffer+3, p_buffer, 0xFFFFFFFD) → massive OOB read into buffer+3 (0x13) → guaranteed fault/corruption. Also memcpy(dma_q->ps_head, p_buffer + buffer_size - 3, 3) reads at p_buffer - 3 (buffer_size=0) → 3-byte under-read OOB.

So the mechanism: completion-time metadata urb->actual_length consumed as copy length and subtraction operand (buffer_size - 3, u32 wrap) without cross-checking >= 3 against header size. This is exactly the hci-completed-length-trust branch. Trigger: malicious/compromised USB device (cx231xx-based, e.g. Pixelview, Conexant) doing a short bulk completion — device firmware is the untrusted source. Impact: heap OOB read of ~4GB and OOB write via kmalloc(0) ZERO_SIZE_PTR, kernel crash/DoS, memory corruption.

Note also kmalloc(buffer_size) with buffer_size = 0: for GFP_ATOMIC kmalloc(0) returns ZERO_SIZE_PTR which is non-NULL → passes the !buffer check.

